 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the civil service law and the administrative code of the city\nof New York, in relation to the rights and working conditions of emergency\nmedical technicians employed by the New York city fire department",
    "basePrintNo": "S3378",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S3378-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §215, Civ Serv L; add §12-140, NYC Ad Cd",
    "lawSection": "Civil Service Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S3378\n\nSPONSOR: GOUNARDES\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the civil service law and the administrative code of the\ncity of New York, in relation to the rights and working conditions of\nemergency medical technicians employed by the New York city fire depart-\nment\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe bill will establish the rights for emergency medical technicians of\nthe New York city fire department. The bill will require the implementa-\ntion of the procedures set forth to establish reasonable standards of\nlabor, rest, meal breaks and pay during such activities.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill amends and adds a new section 215 to the civil\nservice law, which sets forth the scope of work and limitations of such\nwork for emergency medical technicians that are employed by the Fire\nDepartment of the City of New York. The section details the requirement\nof periods devoted to rest, periods devoted to meals and disclosures\nowed during pay statements for emergency medical technicians that work a\nqualifying number of hours per shift. The section also details the\nexceptions to the requirements.\n\nSection two of the bill amends and adds a new section 12-140 to the\nadministrative code of the City of New York. The section details the\nrequirement of periods devoted to rest, periods devoted to meals and\ndisclosures owed during pay statements for emergency medical technicians\nthat work a qualifying number of hours per shift. The section also\ndetails the exceptions to the requirements.\n\nSection three of the bill sets forth the effective date as the imme-\ndiately.\n\n \nEXISTING LAW:\n\nCurrently, there is no law ensuring the rights and fair conditions of\nemergency medical technicians that work for the New York city fire\ndepartment.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nEmergency medical technicians should be recognized and valued equally as\nother uniformed services. The nature of the job that emergency medical\ntechnicians perform is demanding and is quite difficult.  Besides the\nperformance of the emergency medical technicians, the environment in\nwhich they must act is not always predictable. Often, the workers are on\ncall, awaiting an emergency request or are responding to emergency\nrequests for the entire duration of their shift. This bill looks to\nadequately address the issues of meal breaks, rest periods and pay\nrequirements of these hard working New Yorkers that are, at the moment,\ntreated unequally compared to other uniformed services.\n\nEmergency medical technicians provide critical, necessary services to\nthe communities they serve and they deserve to have set rights and limi-\ntations that justly recognize them as vital pillars of our New York\ncommunity and as equals to the other uniformed services of New York.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2018: S8432 - Referred to Civil Service and Pensions.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nUndetermined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
